WEBVTT

00:17.100 --> 00:19.367
- [Al Hibbs  ] We are now
well into the countdown

00:19.367 --> 00:23.334
at approximately three
minutes before liftoff.

00:24.167 --> 00:26.233
All systems are reported
in excellent condition

00:26.233 --> 00:27.699
at this time.

00:28.933 --> 00:30.099
- [Voiceover] Pressurization.Go Atlas.

00:28.933 --> 00:30.100
- [Voiceover] Go Centaur.

00:30.099 --> 00:31.766
- Atlas autopilot, go.

00:31.766 --> 00:32.933
Centaur autopilot, go.

00:32.932 --> 00:34.098
Launch director, go.

00:34.099 --> 00:38.465
(rocket roars)

00:45.998 --> 00:47.565
The Atlas looks good.

00:47.565 --> 00:48.898
Doppler readout
is nominal.

00:48.898 --> 00:50.931
Chamber pressure looks good.

00:50.931 --> 00:54.697
Liftoff was at 7:41
Pacific Daylight Time

00:54.698 --> 00:58.598
on an azimuth of 102 degrees.

00:58.931 --> 01:00.697
The aerodynamic shroud is off

01:00.697 --> 01:03.797
and Grand Bahama is tracking.

01:04.464 --> 01:08.797
Liftoff plus four
minutes and three seconds

01:08.797 --> 01:11.464
as shown by the animated
diagrams in the monitor

01:11.464 --> 01:15.997
the atlas centaur
separation has occurred.

01:17.997 --> 01:18.530
Centaur cut off.

01:18.530 --> 01:22.430
Surveyor is injected on
its lunar flight path.

01:22.796 --> 01:24.063
Separation.

01:24.063 --> 01:27.696
and report that the landing
legs are extended.

01:27.696 --> 01:30.762
The Surveyor is now in space.

01:36.529 --> 01:38.062
- [Voiceover] 63
hours after launch,

01:38.062 --> 01:42.995
Surveyor 1 was only 1000
miles away from the moon.

01:42.996 --> 01:43.762
With the speed increasing

01:43.762 --> 01:48.295
as the moon’s gravitational
attraction beckoned.

01:51.028 --> 01:52.094
-[Voiceover] Vernier ignition.

01:52.095 --> 01:53.128
Retro is now firing.

01:53.128 --> 01:55.295
Ignition looks stable.

01:56.994 --> 01:58.327
Falling steadily.

01:59.094 --> 02:02.627
63,000 feet, 3500 MPH.

02:05.927 --> 02:07.027
Now at 30,000 feet.

02:07.027 --> 02:08.594
Retro burnout confirmed.

02:08.593 --> 02:10.160
- The vehicle was
supposed to enter

02:10.160 --> 02:14.327
a fixed rate of descent.

02:14.326 --> 02:16.626
So that happens.

02:17.226 --> 02:19.026
- [Hibbs] 28,000 feet

02:19.226 --> 02:21.093
425 feet per second.

02:21.092 --> 02:22.392
24,000 feet

02:22.392 --> 02:24.158
falling steadily.

02:24.825 --> 02:26.425
Down to 12,000 feet.

02:26.892 --> 02:28.692
10,000, all signals normal.

02:28.692 --> 02:31.092
Surveyor reported in
excellent condition.

02:31.091 --> 02:33.191
All signals good.

02:33.191 --> 02:34.557
1000 foot mark.

02:35.191 --> 02:36.357
800 feet.

02:37.424 --> 02:38.190
600.

02:38.423 --> 02:40.290
400 feet.

02:40.390 --> 02:42.323
200 feet.

02:42.789 --> 02:45.689
100 feet, 13 feet
per second speed.

02:45.689 --> 02:46.789
- [Suggs] Certain distance
above the surface

02:46.789 --> 02:49.722
the engines are all
supposed to shut off.

02:49.722 --> 02:52.955
And then it’s supposed
to drop to the surface.

02:53.022 --> 02:54.689
The engines all shut off.

03:01.922 --> 03:04.288
And you could hear a pin drop.

03:07.155 --> 03:08.255
It worked.

03:08.255 --> 03:09.621
We’re down.

03:09.987 --> 03:11.620
It’s still transmitting.

03:11.621 --> 03:12.588
(Cheering)

03:12.587 --> 03:15.887
- [Voiceover] Surveyor
1 touched down at 5 MPH.

03:15.887 --> 03:19.920
A mere one second off the
intended landing time.

03:19.920 --> 03:21.686
- [Hibbs] The moon
behind it is very dark.

03:21.687 --> 03:22.687
- [Voiceover] For
the first time,

03:22.687 --> 03:25.854
an American spacecraft had
achieved a soft landing

03:25.853 --> 03:28.120
on another celestial body.

03:28.120 --> 03:30.953
(garbled radio cheering)

03:30.953 --> 03:33.653
Surveyor 1 operated
for six weeks.

03:33.653 --> 03:35.520
Surprising engineers
by surviving

03:35.519 --> 03:38.252
a bitter, cold lunar night.

03:39.852 --> 03:41.852
In all, the sturdy
lander sent back

03:41.852 --> 03:43.819
more than 11,000 images before

03:43.819 --> 03:46.552
its batteries ceased to work.

03:48.718 --> 03:53.551
Surveyor 1 now rests
where it landed in 1966.

03:53.918 --> 03:54.684
Telling us of its presence

03:54.684 --> 04:00.817
by casting a silent, but long
shadow on the lunar surface.

04:04.284 --> 04:05.617
Six more Surveyors were launched

04:05.617 --> 04:09.083
over the next two years,
providing a treasure trove

04:09.084 --> 04:11.651
of science about the moon.

04:11.850 --> 04:13.616
Although two of the
missions would fail,

04:13.617 --> 04:16.484
the increasing ratio
of success to failure

04:16.483 --> 04:19.050
demonstrated that JPL
was clearly learning

04:19.050 --> 04:21.683
from its past mistakes.

04:23.516 --> 04:26.582
And NASA had the
answer to its question.

04:27.082 --> 04:29.848
The lunar surface was
solid and suitable

04:29.849 --> 04:36.016
for landing by the
Apollo astronauts.
